Sawdust Velvet

Ingredients: THRASH METAL + NEO / NU SOUL + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Saxophone  
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a clandestine, truce-sealing wedding between two rival circus dynasties.

Sawdust Velvet is an intoxicating, whiplash-inducing contradiction: the bone-splintering gallop of palm-muted thrash riffs colliding with the syrupy, laid-back pocket of D’Angelo-esque drum displacement and warm Rhodes chords, all warped through hallucinatory tape phasers. The tenor saxophone serves as the volatile mediator—alternating between smoky, microtonal soul crooning and overblown, screeching free-jazz runs that match the high-gain guitar squeals.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 88–96 BPM (felt in deep half-time swing) or a double-time thrash burst at 180+ BPM; the groove constantly drags behind the beat before violently snapping into hyper-speed skank beats.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor 9th and diminished 7th chords slammed against chromatic Locrian riffs, utilizing Phrygian dominant flourishes and detuned, warm analog dissonance.
- Production Signatures: Space-echo dub delays applied directly to guttural, palm-muted rhythm guitars; close-mic'd breathy vocal stacks drenched in reverse reverb; saxophone running through a high-gain fuzz pedal and a Leslie rotary cabinet.
- Dynamics: Louche, smoky, candlelit verses that simmer with low-end Rhodes and fingerpicked bass, abruptly detonating into claustrophobic, high-velocity metal breakdowns that dissolve into melting, psychedelic ambient tails.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound germinated in the damp winter quarters of Baraboo, Wisconsin, when the high-wire aerialist clan of the Vane Family agreed to wed the fire-eating jugglers of the House of Orlov. To keep the rival factions from drawing blades during the reception, the combined house band was ordered to bridge the gap: the Orlovs' ferociously fast touring thrash players were forced to sit in with the Vanes' silk-and-velvet lounge sextet. Held inside a faded canvas tent reeking of paraffin and elephant straw, the makeshift ensemble discovered that a dropped-D chug sounded ungodly when swung behind a buttery J Dilla groove. The scene quickly migrated to disused carnival warehouses across the Rust Belt, birthing an insular subculture where patched denim battle vests are cut from crushed maroon velvet, eyeliner is drawn like greasepaint scars, and fans drink bitter chicory coffee spiked with grain alcohol.

Typical Instruments
- Central Saxophone: Vintage Conn 10M Tenor Saxophone (run through Electro-Harmonix Pitch Fork, Dunlop Cry Baby Wah, and vintage Roland RE-201 Space Echo)
- Rhythm & Harmony: Fender Rhodes Mark I through a cranked Marshall JCM800; Ibanez 7-string guitars tuned to Drop A; Fender Precision Bass with flatwound strings muted with foam
- Percussion & Textures: Deadened acoustic drum kit with heavily dampened snare, Paiste 2002 cymbals, Mellotron M400 (flute and choir tapes), and Buchla 200e modular synth for morphing psychedelic textures

For Fans Of: Flying Lotus, Mr. Bungle, Hiatus Kaiyote, Power Trip

